  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Question": "When writing the equation of a tangent line, which point must be used in the point-slope form?",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "The point of tangency",
    "B": "The origin, regardless of where the point of tangency is",
    "C": "Any point unrelated to the curve",
    "D": "The focus"
  },
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "The tangent line passes through the point of tangency, and its slope is the derivative at that point.\n\nIf the question means the point guaranteed to lie on the tangent, the intended choice is A. However, point-slope form can use any known point on that line. For example, the graph \\(y=0\\) is its own tangent at \\((0,0)\\), but using \\((1,0)\\) also gives \\(y-0=0(x-1)\\). Thus A is the intended choice under that interpretation; the point of tangency need not be the point used.",
